.TH sane\-plustek8200i 5 "07 Oct 2026" "@PACKAGEVERSION@" "SANE Scanner Access Now Easy"
.IX sane\-plustek8200i
.SH NAME
sane\-plustek8200i \- SANE backend for the Plustek OpticFilm 8200i (GL128)
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B sane\-plustek8200i
library implements a SANE (Scanner Access Now Easy) backend that provides
access to the Genesys GL128 based Plustek OpticFilm 8200i film scanner:
.PP
.RS
.ft CR
.nf
Vendor  Product  Chipset  Connection
------  -------  -------  ----------
0x07b3  0x1825   GL128    USB
.fi
.ft R
.RE
.PP
.B Note:
an older scanner sells under the same model name but reports USB product ID
.BR 0x130d ,
and uses a GL845. That one is driven by
.BR sane\-genesys (5),
not by this backend. Identify the device by its USB ID or chipset rather
than by its model name.
.PP
This is a transparency-only scanner: there is no flatbed mode. The device
has a fourth, infrared illuminant that this backend uses to detect and
repair dust and scratches.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-mode Color|Gray|Infrared|Color+Infrared
Selects the scan mode.
.B Color
delivers RGB and
.B Gray
a single averaged channel.
.B Infrared
delivers only the infrared channel, in which film dye is transparent, so
that physical defects alone remain.
.B Color+Infrared
delivers two SANE frames: the colour image, then its pixel-aligned
infrared plane as a gray frame. SANE has no standard four-channel frame
format, so a frontend must accept multiple frames to use it;
.BR scanimage (1)
cannot save the second frame.
.TP
.B \-\-resolution 300|600|1200|2400|3600|7200
Scan resolution in dpi. Above roughly 1490 dpi the vertical sampling is
set by the line rate against the motor speed, so the higher settings are
upsampled vertically to the resolution they report.
.TP
.B \-\-depth 8|16
Bits per channel. The sensor is 16 bit; 8 bit keeps the high byte of each
sample.
.TP
.B \-\-ir\-clean[=yes|no]
Make an additional infrared pass and repair dust and scratches in the
colour image from it. This roughly doubles the scan time.
.TP
.B \-\-auto\-wb[=yes|no]
Scale the colour channels so that their means match. Off by default,
because negative conversion and colour profiling need the sensor's
calibrated response unchanged.
.TP
.B \-\-exposure 1.0..4.0
Light integration time as a multiple of the default. The carriage slows
down to preserve the geometry, so the scan takes proportionally longer.
For dense negatives and dark slides.
.TP
.B \-l \-t \-x \-y
The scan window, in millimetres, within the film frame.
.SH IMAGE DATA
As required by the SANE standard, 16 bit samples are delivered in the
.B native byte order of the host
the backend runs on. Frontends that write a fixed-endian file format must
byte-swap accordingly, and
.BR saned (8)
reconciles the byte order itself when the client and server differ.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
The backend scans one frame per
.BR sane_start .
It does not advance the film or select a frame: position the holder by
hand, then scan. There is no batch mode.
.IP \(bu 2
The scanner takes 35 mm film only, strips and mounted slides; no other
film size exists for it, so there is nothing to select. The largest scan
window, which the backend covers entirely, is about
36.5 x 27.2 mm, the frame plus a margin at each end. The
.B \-l \-t \-x \-y
options select a part of that window.
.IP \(bu 2
The resolutions are those listed under
.BR \-\-resolution ;
there is nothing below 300 dpi. A 7200 dpi scan is about 10368 x 7755
pixels and takes roughly two minutes. Above about 1490 dpi the film is
sampled more finely by slowing the carriage, so scans at the highest
settings take proportionally longer.
.IP \(bu 2
The image is delivered as the sensor measured it, after shading
correction. The backend does not invert negatives, apply a gamma curve or
attach a colour profile; the frontend has to.
.IP \(bu 2
The scanner is calibrated before every scan, and the result is not
cached. This adds to the scan time but keeps the calibration current.
.IP \(bu 2
Infrared dust and scratch repair
.RB ( \-\-ir\-clean )
applies to colour scans only.
.B Color+Infrared
needs a frontend that accepts more than one SANE frame per image.
.IP \(bu 2
Infrared repair does not work on Kodachrome slides. Its dyes absorb
infrared as they absorb visible light, so the infrared plane shows the
picture as well as the dust, and
.B \-\-ir\-clean
cannot tell the two apart and can damage the image. The backend does not
know the film type and does not warn: do not use
.B \-\-ir\-clean
or the infrared plane for dust removal on Kodachrome, and remove dust in
software instead. Ektachrome and other E-6 slide films, and colour
negatives, are transparent to infrared and work normally. Silver
black and white negatives block infrared as well.
.IP \(bu 2
Non-blocking I/O is not supported:
.BR sane_set_io_mode
refuses it and
.BR sane_get_select_fd
returns
.BR SANE_STATUS_UNSUPPORTED .
.IP \(bu 2
The backend was developed and verified against a single scanner. The
image geometry (the vertical scale, the horizontal mirroring and the
margins) was fitted against scans made with the vendor's own software,
and another unit or hardware revision may need adjustments. The older
OpticFilm 8200i with USB product ID
.B 0x130d
is not handled by this backend.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @CONFIGDIR@/plustek8200i.conf
The backend configuration file.
.TP
.I @LIBDIR@/libsane\-plustek8200i.a
The static library implementing this backend.
.TP
.I @LIBDIR@/libsane\-plustek8200i.so
The shared library implementing this backend (present on systems that
support dynamic loading).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SANE_DEBUG_PLUSTEK8200I
If the library was compiled with debug support enabled, this variable
controls the debug level: 0 errors, 1 adds warnings, 2 adds progress,
3 adds detail on the scan setup, and 4 adds every register write.
Level 4 is enough to reconstruct the register state at any point of a
scan without a USB sniffer.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR sane (7),
.BR sane\-genesys (5),
.BR sane\-usb (5),
.BR scanimage (1),
.BR saned (8)
